WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.824 --> 00:00:03.890
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Stephanie,
what are you doing?

00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:06.098
STEPHANIE WONG: I'm manually
making a copy of my data

00:00:06.098 --> 00:00:07.620
in case my database goes down.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.090
MARK MIRCHANDANI: You don't
need to do that by hand.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.460
Here, let me show you how
to set up high availability.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:11.670
STEPHANIE WONG: All right.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:12.295
Thank goodness.

00:00:19.482 --> 00:00:21.190
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Let's take a quick look

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:23.740
at what happens with a highly
available configuration using

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:24.940
MySQL.

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.100
The primary instance
and failover replicas

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:28.810
are in two separate zones.

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:30.760
As updates are made to
the primary instance,

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.218
they're replicated
to the failover.

00:00:32.218 --> 00:00:34.240
If the primary
instance goes down,

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.730
Cloud SQL will make sure
the failover is up-to-date,

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:39.350
and then promote it to
the primary instance.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:41.590
This is what it looks like
when the failover happens.

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:44.150
Instead of manually making
any application changes,

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:46.600
Cloud SQL moves the
primary IP address over,

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.979
so the same database connection
information will work.

00:00:48.979 --> 00:00:50.770
It looks a little
different for PostgreSQL,

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:53.290
but you still get
seamless failover.

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:55.980
STEPHANIE WONG: So not only does
Cloud SQL manage my database,

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:57.730
it can actually handle
my primary database

00:00:57.730 --> 00:00:58.510
going down too?

00:00:58.510 --> 00:00:59.170
MARK MIRCHANDANI: That's right.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:01.211
Now that we've seen what
happens in the back end,

00:01:01.211 --> 00:01:02.800
let's take a look
at how to set it up.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.030
By going to the Cloud
SQL Instances page,

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:07.000
we can our currently
running databases.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.090
Looking at Needs Failover,
you can click the Add button

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:13.180
under High Availability to set
up an automatically managed

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:14.230
replica.

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:17.230
We'll go ahead and call it
Needs Failover Failover,

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:19.040
and hit the Create button.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.010
This will create the
failover for you.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:23.362
Now, if we're going to
create a new database,

00:01:23.362 --> 00:01:24.820
the first thing we
can do is choose

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:30.080
between MySQL or PostgreSQL So
we'll click on Create Instance.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:33.302
We'll choose MySQL, which
is second generation.

00:01:33.302 --> 00:01:35.260
And we're going to go
ahead and give it a name.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.700
We'll call it MyNewDB.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:40.940
Don't forget to secure it
with a secure root password.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:43.400
Then, we'll go to the
high availability section,

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:45.230
and we'll make sure
binary logging--

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:46.370
this is for MySQL--

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:50.900
is checked off, and click
Create Failover Replica.

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:52.950
We'll go ahead and then
click the Create button,

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.050
and we're good to go.

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:57.560
Now, once these
failovers are created,

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:01.070
the other thing we're able
to do is test out a failover.

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:03.810
So if we actually go to one
that already has it set up,

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:06.620
click in on that, and
then go to the top right

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:08.090
where it says failover.

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:11.192
This will let you actually
execute a failover.

00:02:11.192 --> 00:02:13.400
Now you can see how easy it
is to make your Cloud SQL

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:14.790
instances highly available.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:16.540
STEPHANIE WONG: So if
the primary instance

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:19.250
is down due to being patched,
or for any other reason,

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:21.401
we have a replica set
up and ready to go.

00:02:21.401 --> 00:02:23.400
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Don't
forget to like, comment,

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.950
and subscribe for more great
Google Cloud Platform content.

